;;; tur/forms/types -- converting, ascribing and hiding types: as, ::, cast, forall, exists, borrows.
;;;
;;; These forms move values between types, or say something about a type
;;; instead of computing a value. They fall into groups that are easy to confuse:
;;;
;;;   as                   convert a number to another numeric kind (changes the value)
;;;   ::                   say a value has another type, without converting (changes the label)
;;;   any: ::, cast, is?, type-of
;;;                        put a value in a tagged box and take it back out by its tag
;;;   ?                    return early from a function on a failed Result
;;;   coerce               use an `Equal` proof to treat a value as another type
;;;   type-app             removed -- write the applied type (Option int)
;;;   forall, exists, pack, open
;;;                        polymorphic function arguments, and values whose type is hidden
;;;   &, &mut              borrow a value without taking it
;;;
;;; `as` and `::` are the pair to keep apart: `(as float 7)` is the number 7 as a
;;; float, `(:: n Meters)` is the same bits under another name. Integer and float
;;; kinds cannot be ascribed to each other at all -- the compiler cannot tell which
;;; of the two a program means, so it stops (see `::`). Several forms here differ
;;; between the compiler and the interpreter, or do not do what the guides say;
;;; each entry names its report or plan.
;; Reference only: tools/gendocs.py reads the ;;@ lines, the compiler skips them.

;;; as -- convert a number to another numeric kind.
;;;
;;; The first argument is a bare numeric type name, not evaluated: `int`, `int8`
;;; ... `int64`, `uint8` ... `uint64`, `float`, `float32`, `float64` (a keyword
;;; such as `:int8` is `'as' expects a type name as first argument`, and a
;;; non-numeric target such as `bool` is `'as' casts are only valid for numeric
;;; types`). Arithmetic does not mix widths implicitly, so `(+ 1i8 (as int8
;;; 2i16))` is how a narrower or wider operand is brought to match.
;;;
;;; Integer to integer keeps the low bits: `(as int8 300)` is 44 and `(as uint8
;;; -1)` is 255. Integer to float is the nearest float, and a float to an integer
;;; discards the fraction toward zero: `(as int 7.9)` is 7 and `(as int -7.9)` is
;;; -7. A float outside the range of the integer it is converted to has no
;;; defined answer, and the two engines differ: `(as uint8 300.7)` is 255 compiled
;;; (it saturates) and 44 interpreted. Keep the value in range first.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   type -- the numeric kind to convert to, as a bare name
;;;   expr -- the number to convert
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value as the named kind.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (as int 7.9)  ; => 7
;;;   (as uint8 -1)  ; => 255
;;;   (as float32 7.1)  ; => 7.1
;;@form (as type expr)

;;; :: -- give a value another type without converting it.
;;;
;;; `(:: expr Type)` is a type ascription. The type is a keyword or a bare name
;;; (`:int`, `int`, `Meters`, `(Option float)`, `ptr<void>`, `any`). It moves a
;;; value across a boundary the checker would otherwise stop at, and never
;;; changes the bits: `(:: n Meters)` makes an `int` into a `defopaque` newtype and
;;; `(:: m :int)` takes it back; `(:: true :int)` is 1 and `(:: 1 :bool)` is
;;; `true`; an integer literal given a float type becomes that float
;;; (`(:: 3 :float)` is 3.0). Nothing is wrapped or rounded -- `(:: 300 :int8)` is
;;; still 300; use `as` for a conversion.
;;;
;;; An ascription between an integer kind and a float kind is a compile error
;;; because it could mean convert or reinterpret: use `float->int` /
;;; `int->float` (stdlib/math.tur) to convert and `float->bits` (stdlib/bits.tur)
;;; to read the bits. An aggregate passed by value (a `^copy` struct) cannot be
;;; ascribed to `:int` or `ptr<void>` (TUR-E0295): box it into `any`. An `any`
;;; cannot be ascribed back to a type -- `::` asserts a representation and an
;;; `any` is a tagged pair -- so use `cast`, which checks the tag. Ascribing a
;;; string to `:int` yields its address compiled and leaves the string
;;; interpreted; do not rely on either.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   expr -- the value
;;;   type -- the type it is to have
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   `expr`, unchanged, at the new type.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (:: true :int)  ; => 1
;;;   (:: 1 :bool)  ; => true
;;;   (:: 7.1 :float32)  ; => 7.1
;;@form (:: expr type)

;;; ? -- return early from a function when a Result (or Option) is an error.
;;;
;;; `(? expr)`, or `?(expr)` in neoteric syntax, evaluates `expr` once. If it is
;;; an error the enclosing function returns it; otherwise the `?` is the ok value.
;;; It is written inside a function body (`? operator is only allowed inside a
;;; function body`) and the function must itself return the same kind of value.
;;; A non-Result operand such as `(? 5)` is TUR-E0001.
;;;
;;; An operand statically typed `(Result T E)` -- a variable, or a call of a named
;;; function that returns one -- lowers to `(if (err? q) (return (Err (err-val q)))
;;; (ok-val q))`, so the error type must agree and the ok type may differ; a
;;; `(Option T)` operand lowers the same way with `None` and `unwrap`. That works
;;; on every engine. Any other operand (a call whose result type is not known
;;; without elaborating it, or a Result carried as a `ptr<void>`) takes the
;;; older route through the `__tur-q-is-err?` / `__tur-q-ok-val` helpers in
;;; stdlib/result.tur, which read the pointer layout and do not run interpreted;
;;; bind such a value to a typed variable first.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   expr -- a Result or Option value
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The ok payload; the error is returned from the function instead.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defn halve [n : int] : (Result int cstr) (if (= (mod n 2) 0) (ok (/ n 2)) (err "odd")))
;;;   (defn quarter [n : int] : (Result int cstr) (let [h (? (halve n))] (halve h)))
;;@form (? expr)

;;; cast -- take a value out of an any, checking its type.
;;;
;;; An `any` carries a value and a tag naming its type. `(cast x Type)` reads the
;;; value back as `Type` if the tag matches, and panics otherwise
;;; (`cast: any holds float, not int`). `x` must be an `any`; a plain value is
;;; `'cast' expects an 'any'-typed first argument`, and an unknown `Type` is
;;; `unknown type 'Nope' in 'cast'`. A parametrised type is written applied,
;;; `(cast x (Option int))`; the bare constructor `Option` is an error.
;;; Test first with `is?` to avoid the panic.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   x    -- an `any`
;;;   type -- the type to read it as
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value, at `type`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (cast (:: 7.1 any) float)  ; => 7.1
;;;   (cast (:: "hi" any) cstr)  ; => "hi"
;;@form (cast x type)

;;; is? -- test what type an any is carrying.
;;;
;;; `(is? x Type)` is true when the `any` `x` was made from a value of type
;;; `Type`. `x` must be an `any` (`'is?' expects an 'any'-typed first argument`
;;; otherwise). A parametrised type is written applied: `(is? x (Option int))`
;;; is true for `(:: (Some 3) any)` and `(is? x (Option float))` is false
;;; compiled, while the interpreter, which does not record the instantiation, says
;;; true for both. Sized numbers differ the same way: `(is? (:: 3i32 any) int32)`
;;; is true compiled and false interpreted.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   x    -- an `any`
;;;   type -- the type to test for
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   `true` if `x` holds a value of that type, else `false`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (is? (:: 7.1 any) float)  ; => true
;;;   (is? (:: 42 any) float)  ; => false
;;@form (is? x type)

;;; type-of -- the name of the type an any is carrying.
;;;
;;; Takes an `any` and returns the type's name as a string: `float`, `int`, `cstr`,
;;; `bool`, `Sym` for a keyword, `fn` for a function, the type name of a struct
;;; or ADT (`Option` without its instantiation). A value that is not already an
;;; `any` is `'type-of' expects an 'any'-typed argument`. A sized integer is
;;; named for its size when compiled (`int32` for `(:: 3i32 any)`, `uint8`, ...);
;;; the interpreter has one integer and one float kind and says `int` / `float`.
;;; A `float32` is widened as a float, so it reads `float` on both. See `is?`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   x -- an `any`
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The type's name, a `cstr`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (type-of (:: 7.1 any))  ; => "float"
;;;   (type-of (:: "hi" any))  ; => "cstr"
;;@builtin (type-of [x : any] : cstr)

;;; coerce -- use an Equal proof to treat a value as another type.
;;;
;;; `(coerce proof x)` evaluates `proof`, which must be a value of the `Equal`
;;; type (`(Refl)` after `(load "stdlib/equal.tur")`, or a parameter annotated
;;; with the bare name `Equal` or applied, `(Equal int int)`), and returns `x`.
;;; Nothing is converted and no code is emitted. With `proof : (Equal a b)` and
;;; `x : a`, the result has type `b`: `(defn conv [A B] [eq : (Equal A B) x : A]
;;; : B (coerce eq x))` type-checks, and `(conv (Refl) 7.1)` is a float -- the
;;; call reads `B = A` from `Refl`'s `(Equal a a)`. With the bare `Equal`
;;; annotation the result keeps `x`'s own type. An applied proof checks that its
;;; `a` is `x`'s type when both are concrete (`coerce: the proof is about a value
;;; of type ...`), and a proof relating two different concrete types, `(Equal int
;;; float)`, is `coerce: the proof (Equal int float) relates different types` --
;;; `Refl` cannot be built there. A proof `(Equal A int)` with a type-variable
;;; `a` and a concrete `b` still returns `x` at `A`. A session channel cannot be
;;; coerced. A first argument that is not an `Equal` is `coerce requires an
;;; (Equal a b) proof`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   proof -- an `Equal` value
;;;   x     -- the value
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   `x`, at the proof's `b`.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (load "stdlib/equal.tur")
;;;   (println (coerce (Refl) 42))  ; prints 42
;;;   (defn conv [A B] [eq : (Equal A B) x : A] : B (coerce eq x))
;;;   (println (conv (Refl) 7.1))   ; prints 7.1
;;@form (coerce proof x)

;;; type-app -- removed: write the applied type `(F A)`.
;;;
;;; `(type-app F A)` used to be the explicit spelling of applying the type
;;; constructor `F` to the type `A`. In a type annotation it never worked (it was
;;; TUR-E0012 `cannot apply a type of kind '*' as a type constructor` for every
;;; constructor), and as a statement it checked an arity and did nothing. It is
;;; now an error in every position that names the spelling to use instead:
;;; `(type-app Option int)` is `type-app has been removed: write the applied type
;;; (Option int)`. The word stays reserved for a release. Messages print a type
;;; application the same way, `(Option float)`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   F -- a type constructor
;;;   A -- the type argument
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defn size [o : (Option int)] : int 0)  ; not (type-app Option int)
;;@form (type-app F A)

;;; forall -- a type that works for every type: polymorphic arguments.
;;;
;;; Appears in a type, never as an expression. In a parameter annotation,
;;; `[f (forall [a] (-> a a)) x : int]`, it makes `f` a rank-2 function: the
;;; callee may use `f` at any type it likes, so `(f x)` with an `int` and `(f s)`
;;; with a `cstr` in the same body both type-check, and the caller must pass a
;;; function that is generic in that way (hrt-guide). Several variables go in the
;;; bracket, `(forall [a b] (-> a b a))`, and a typeclass constraint after them,
;;; `(forall [a] [(Show a)] (-> a cstr))`; a bound variable can carry a kind,
;;; `(forall [(f :: * -> *)] ...)`. The argument must be a named function or a
;;; lambda that captures nothing (a capturing one is `rank-2 argument must be a
;;; named function`); a non-function is `expected ptr<void>, got int`; and a poly
;;; function cannot be stored in a list or option.
;;;
;;; The function passed must be generic wherever the forall body has a bare bound
;;; variable: its parameter there is a type parameter (`[A] [x : A]`) or
;;; un-annotated -- not `any`, a tagged value the untyped word cannot fill
;;; (TUR-E0001 says so). Passing `inc : int -> int` where `(forall [a] (-> a a))` is
;;; expected is `rank-2 argument 'inc' is not generic` (TUR-E0001), for a named
;;; function, a lambda, or a function defined later in the file alike. When the
;;; callee only ever applies it at the type it handles, ascribe it to the forall
;;; type to say so: `(apply-poly (:: inc (forall [a] (-> a a))) 7)`. The
;;; ascription is trusted, not checked. Not checked yet: the result type, and a
;;; variable under a type application such as `(List a)`.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   [a ...]       -- the bound type variables
;;;   [(C a) ...]   -- optional typeclass constraints on them
;;;   type          -- the type, usually a function type `(-> ...)`
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defn id [x] x)
;;;   (defn apply-poly [f (forall [a] (-> a a)) x : int] : int (f x))
;;;   (println (apply-poly id 7))  ; prints 7
;;@form (forall [a ...] type)

;;; exists -- a type whose concrete part is hidden.
;;;
;;; Appears in a type. `(exists [a] T)` is a value of some type `a` that the
;;; holder cannot name, packaged with whatever `T` says about it, and
;;; `(exists [a] [(Show a)] a)` adds the typeclass operations the holder may use on
;;; it. It is the dual of `forall`: there the caller chooses the type, here the
;;; maker does. Values are made with `pack` and used with `open`; a function can
;;; take one, `[e : (exists [a] [(Show a)] a)]`, or return one. Values of different
;;; types that share the class can then be handled alike (existential-types-guide).
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   [a ...]       -- the hidden type variables
;;;   [(C a) ...]   -- optional typeclass constraints on them
;;;   type          -- the type of the packed value, in terms of the variables
;;@form (exists [a ...] type)

;;; pack -- hide a value's type inside an existential.
;;;
;;; `(pack value (exists [a] [(C a)] a))` makes the existential. At the pack site
;;; the compiler checks that the value's type has an instance for each constraint
;;; (`pack: no instance 'Shape' for type 'int'` otherwise); the result carries the
;;; value and the evidence for the constraints, so values of different types can
;;; be treated alike afterwards. Without constraints, `(pack 42 (exists [a] a))`,
;;; nothing can be done with the value but hide it. The `(pack int 0)` spelling
;;; in hrt-guide is not accepted.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   value -- the value to hide
;;;   type  -- the `exists` type to give it
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The existential value.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (defclass Show [a] (show [x] : cstr))
;;;   (definstance Show [int] (show [x] : cstr "an int"))
;;;   (defn main [] : int
;;;     (let [e (pack 42 (exists [a] [(Show a)] a))]
;;;       (open e [_ v] (println (show v))))  ; prints an int
;;;     0)
;;@form (pack value type)

;;; open -- use the value inside an existential.
;;;
;;; `(open e [a v] body)` unpacks the existential `e`: inside `body`, `v` is the
;;; hidden value, `a` is its type as an abstract name that matches only itself,
;;; and the class operations promised by the type work on `v`: `(show v)` or
;;; `(.show v)`. The binder for the type can be `_`. The result of `body` may not
;;; mention `a`, so the value cannot leave: returning `v` itself is `open:
;;; existential type variable 'a' escapes its scope`. The unpacking uses the
;;; evidence packed with the value to choose the operation, so two packed values
;;; of different types each reach their own instance -- two `defopaque` types
;;; over `:int` included, on every engine.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   e    -- an existential value
;;;   [a v] -- names for the hidden type and the value
;;;   body -- the expression to evaluate with them
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   The value of `body`, which may not have the hidden type.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (open (pack 42 (exists [a] a)) [a v] 7)  ; => 7
;;@form (open e [a v] body)

;;; & -- borrow a value without taking it.
;;;
;;; `(& place)`, written `&place`, makes an immutable borrow of type `&T`: a
;;; reference to a variable, a field (`&(.x p)`) or another borrow (a reborrow is
;;; still `&T`). Read through it with `@r`; a borrow is not itself a number, so
;;; `(println r)` is a type error and `(println @r)` prints the value. Any number
;;; of immutable borrows of one variable may exist together. A parameter is typed
;;; `r : &int`. The checker rejects a function that returns a borrow of its own
;;; local (TUR-E0105). A borrow lasts to the end of the enclosing `let`, so
;;; borrowing `x` mutably afterwards in that same `let` is `cannot borrow 'x' as
;;; mutable while it is already borrowed`; close the `let` first.
;;;
;;; While a `let` holds a borrow of a place, `set!` of that place is refused if
;;; the borrow is used after the assignment (`cannot assign to 'x' while it is
;;; borrowed by 'r'`); a borrow the body is done with does not block the write.
;;; A borrow of a literal (`(& 7.25)`) borrows a temporary that lives as long as
;;; the `let` holding it.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   place -- a variable, a field of one, or a borrow
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   A `&T` reference.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (let [x 41] (let [r &x] @r))  ; => 41
;;@form (& place)

;;; &mut -- borrow a value so it can be changed through the borrow.
;;;
;;; `(&mut place)`, written `&mut place`, makes an exclusive borrow of type `&mut T`.
;;; Write through it with `(set! @r value)`; reading is `@r`. While it lives no
;;; other borrow of the same variable may be made, mutable or not (`cannot borrow
;;; 'x' as immutable while it is mutably borrowed`), and the variable itself is
;;; not changed behind its back. A parameter is typed `r : &mut int`, so a helper
;;; can change its caller's variable: `(defn bump [r : &mut int] : nil (set! @r
;;; (+ @r 1)))` called as `(bump &mut x)`.
;;;
;;; The place must be one `set!` could write: a `^mut` variable or a field of
;;; one (`cannot borrow 'x' as mutable: it is not declared '^mut'`). A place
;;; behind an `rc`, a `^heap` struct or another borrow needs nothing.
;;;
;;; Parameters:
;;;   place -- a variable, or a field of one
;;;
;;; Returns:
;;;   A `&mut T` reference.
;;;
;;; Example:
;;;   (let [^mut y 5] (let [m &mut y] (set! @m 42)) y)  ; => 42
;;@form (&mut place)
